Bill Division (Bon Appétit)

Two friends Anna and Brian are deciding how to split the bill at a dinner. Each person should only pay for the items they actually consumed.

Brian receives the bill and calculates Anna’s share. However, Anna did not eat one of the items, so Brian should not include that item when splitting the bill.

Your task is to determine whether Brian charged Anna correctly.

Example

Suppose the bill is:

bill = [2, 4, 6]

Anna did not eat the item at index k = 2 (which costs 6).

The correct bill Anna should pay:

(2 + 4) / 2 = 3

If Brian incorrectly includes the item she didn’t eat:

(2 + 4 + 6) / 2 = 6

In this case, Brian overcharged Anna by 3, so he should refund 3.

Function Description

Complete the function:

bonAppetit(List<Integer> bill, int k, int b)
Parameters

bill – an array of integers representing the cost of each item ordered

k – the 0-based index of the item that Anna did not eat

b – the amount of money Brian charged Anna

Input Format

The first line contains two integers n and k

n → number of items ordered

k → index of the item Anna didn’t eat

The second line contains n space-separated integers describing the cost of each item.

The third line contains integer b, the amount Brian charged Anna.

Constraints
2 ≤ n ≤ 10^5
0 ≤ k < n
0 ≤ bill[i] ≤ 10^4
0 ≤ b ≤ sum of bill
Output Format

If Brian charged Anna correctly, print:

Bon Appetit

Otherwise, print the amount Brian must refund to Anna.